<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-setup-subsection-chain-rule" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Review of the chain rule for multivariable functions</title>

    <p>
        Consider a function <m>f = f(x,y,z,t)</m>.
        When finding a partial derivative of <m>f</m> in terms of one of its independent variables,
        the other independent variables are held constant.
        For example,
        the independent variables <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>, and <m>t</m> are held constant when finding <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        As discussed in <xref ref="ch1-section-calculus-subsection-partial-differentiation" text="custom">Chapter 1</xref>,
        we can use subscripts to make explicit which independent variables are held constant for a particular partial derivative.
        Returning to the previous example:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
            =
            \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \right)_{y,z,t}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where the subscripts "y", "z", and "t" indicate that these independent variables are held constant
        when taking the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Similar reasoning and notation are used when finding <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial z}</m>,
        and <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial t}</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
            =
            \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \right)_{x,z,t}
        </md> 
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
            =
            \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial z} \right)_{x,y,t}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
            =
            \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} \right)_{x,y,z}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        If <m>x = x(q,r)</m>, <m>y = y(q,r)</m>, <m>z = z(q,r)</m>, and <m>t = t(q,r)</m>,
        then <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>, and <m>t</m> are considered <term>intermediate variables</term>
        and <m>q</m> and <m>r</m> are considered <term>independent variables</term>,
        and we can find <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial q}</m> and <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial r}</m>
        using the chain rule for multivariable functions.
        For example:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-basic" number="yes">
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial q}
            =
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial q}
            +
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial q}
            +
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\frac{\partial z}{\partial q}
            +
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}\frac{\partial t}{\partial q}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-basic" text="type-global" /> should be familiar from your multivariable calculus course.
        But this form hides which intermediate or independent variables are held constant for each partial derivative.
        Although it requires more writing, we can use subscripts to make explicit which variables are held constant for each partial derivative.
        Using this notation, <xref ref="eqn-chain-rule-basic" text="type-global" /> becomes:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial q}
                \right)_{r}
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
                \right)_{y,z,t}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial x}{\partial q}
                \right)_{r}
                +
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
                \right)_{x,z,t}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial y}{\partial q}
                \right)_{r}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-chain-rule-subscript" number="yes">
                \amp +
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}
                \right)_{x,y,t}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial z}{\partial q}
                \right)_{r}
                +
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}
                \right)_{x,y,z}
                \left(
                    \frac{\partial t}{\partial q}
                \right)_{r}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        We will make use of the chain rule for multivariable functions with subscript notation in the next sub-section.
    </p>

</subsection>